  "chooseSection": {
    "heading": "How to Choose a Tiller Alternative",
    "body": "Start with the software environment. Tiller is built strictly for Google Sheets and Microsoft Excel, feeding account balances and transactions into private spreadsheets that you own. Every alternative on this page operates as a standalone cloud application with proprietary dashboards for web, mobile, or desktop.\n\nNext, evaluate the billing commitment and pricing math. Tiller costs $99 per year, which works out to $8.25 per month. Monarch Core costs $99.99 per year, making Tiller $0.99 less per year. On monthly billing, Monarch charges $14.99 per month, which totals $179.88 over 12 months. YNAB costs $109 per year, so Tiller costs $10 less per year. YNAB monthly billing also runs $14.99 per month, totaling $179.88 across 12 months, which is $70.88 higher than its annual rate. Copilot Money charges $109 billed yearly under a limited-time offer, or $9 per month, which totals $108 across 12 payments of $9. EveryDollar Premium costs $79.99 per year, which is $19.01 less than Tiller's $99 annual fee, but its monthly plan costs $17.99 per month ($215.88 over 12 months). Simplifi displays $3.99 per month and $6.99 labeled billed annually, with discounts applying to the first year for orders by December 8, 2026, and a footnote stating the final annual price may differ from the monthly figure multiplied by 12.\n\nThen, compare trial rules. Tiller grants a 30-day free trial without charging your card until the period concludes, followed by a 30-day refund window. YNAB provides a 34-day free trial that requires no credit card when signing up directly on its website. EveryDollar Premium includes a 14-day trial. Monarch provides a 7-day trial. Copilot Money and Simplifi list no specific trial length on their pricing pages.\n\nFinally, check account sharing and platform support. YNAB permits sharing a single subscription among a group of up to six people. Simplifi allows sharing with one other person. Monarch includes partner sharing and lists a Plus plan for multi-source income. Copilot Money lists its Households feature as coming soon and supports iPhone, iPad, Mac, and Web. Tiller relies on sharing permissions configured directly inside your Google Drive or Microsoft account."
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "heading": "Monarch Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Monarch is a personal finance app that operates across web and mobile platforms without third-party advertisements. Monarch publishes two subscription plans: Core at $99.99 per year or $14.99 per month, and Plus at $199.99 per year. A promotional banner advertises 30% off the Core annual plan for the first year with the code WELCOME, subject to terms and expiration. Monarch includes a 7-day free trial, allows cancellation at any time, provides a money-back guarantee, and recommends web signup over mobile app stores. The Plus plan introduces forward-looking forecasting tools, tracking for multiple income sources such as a small business, and deeper investment tracking.\n\nMonarch fits households and solo earners who prefer dedicated web and mobile interfaces over spreadsheets, and who want forecasting or small-business income tracking.\n\nVersus Tiller, Monarch falls short for users who demand direct formula access and custom spreadsheet modeling. Tiller stores your transactions inside Google Sheets or Microsoft Excel workbooks that you control completely. Monarch stores financial records inside its proprietary cloud database. Monarch also provides a 7-day free trial, which is shorter than Tiller's 30-day trial and 30-day refund window.\n\nWorked cost example: Monarch Core on annual billing costs $99.99 per year, which is $0.99 more than Tiller's $99 annual fee. Paying Monarch Core month to month costs $14.99 per month, totaling $179.88 over 12 months. That monthly schedule costs $80.88 more per year than Tiller. Upgrading to Monarch Plus at $199.99 per year costs $100.99 more annually than Tiller."
    },
    {
      "heading": "YNAB Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "YNAB, short for You Need A Budget, organizes household spending around a structured budgeting method where users assign every dollar a job. YNAB publishes two pricing options: an Annual plan at $109 billed annually (an equivalent of $9.08 per month, which YNAB states delivers $70 in savings compared to monthly payments) and a Monthly plan at $14.99 per month, plus applicable sales tax. YNAB provides a 34-day free trial that requires no payment card details when signing up directly on its website, though mobile app stores may require one. College students can obtain a free 365-day trial with verification. A single YNAB subscription can be shared with a group of up to six people, and the software includes debt payoff tools with an integrated loan calculator.\n\nYNAB fits budgeters who want a proactive envelope-style allocation system and who want to share a subscription across up to six family members or partners.\n\nVersus Tiller, YNAB enforces a rigid envelope workflow and lacks spreadsheet extensibility. Tiller leaves the budgeting philosophy entirely to the user, providing raw transaction feeds that can power automated charts, custom pivot tables, or multi-year financial ledgers. YNAB also costs more on annual billing: $109 per year compared to Tiller's $99 per year, a difference of $10.\n\nWorked cost example: YNAB annual billing costs $109 per year, which is $10 more than Tiller's $99 annual rate. Paying YNAB monthly at $14.99 per month costs $179.88 across 12 billing cycles, which is $70.88 more than YNAB's own annual plan and $80.88 more than Tiller's $99 annual cost."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Simplifi Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Simplifi is a cloud-based personal finance application for web and mobile devices, created by Quicken. Simplifi connects with over 14,000 financial institutions and is designed for use in the United States and Canada. Its homepage displays pricing figures of $3.99 per month alongside $6.99 labeled billed annually. A footnote clarifies that discounts apply to the annual price, the final price may differ from the monthly discounted price multiplied by 12, and promotional offers are valid for the first year on new memberships ordered directly by December 8, 2026, renewing automatically at then-current rates. Simplifi provides a dynamic Spending Plan that subtracts bills and savings goals from income, Projected Cash Flow looking up to 12 months ahead, net worth tracking, Watchlist monitors, Savings Goals, a Retirement Planner, Quicken AI, account sharing with one other person, and free one-on-one setup help.\n\nSimplifi fits individuals and couples who want rolling cash flow forecasts up to 12 months in advance, setup assistance from live specialists, and automated spending categories on mobile and web.\n\nVersus Tiller, Simplifi does not support Microsoft Excel or Google Sheets automation, confining your financial data to its proprietary application. Simplifi also restricts geographical use to the United States and Canada, and limits account sharing to one partner. Tiller allows you to share spreadsheets with anyone through standard Google Drive or Microsoft OneDrive permissions.\n\nWorked cost example: Simplifi publishes promotional pricing for orders placed by December 8, 2026, but notes in its terms that discounts apply to the annual rate and that the final annual amount may differ from multiplying the monthly figure by 12. Because Simplifi does not publish an exact consolidated 12-month renewal dollar total on the checked page, an exact multi-year cash comparison against Tiller's flat $99 annual fee cannot be computed from listed regular rates."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Copilot Money Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Copilot Money is an account tracking and budgeting application available on iPhone, iPad, Mac, and Web. Copilot Money publishes a single plan structure with no tiers, no add-on purchases, and no artificial intelligence credits. Its pricing page lists an annual subscription at $109 billed yearly, described as a limited-time offer from its standard $119 price, alongside a $9 per month rate. Copilot Money connects to more than 16,000 financial institutions, includes an embedded money assistant, provides customer support from real humans, and states that it never sells user financial data. A Households feature allowing users to share an account with a partner or family member is marked as coming soon.\n\nCopilot Money fits Apple ecosystem and web users who want automated transaction categorization, machine intelligence support, and a single plan with all features included.\n\nVersus Tiller, Copilot Money does not provide spreadsheet data feeds, supports no Windows or Android desktop clients, and has not yet launched its shared household access. Tiller functions inside any desktop browser that runs Google Sheets or Microsoft Excel, making it accessible across Windows, Mac, and Linux computers.\n\nWorked cost example: At its $109 annual promotional rate, Copilot Money costs $10 more per year than Tiller's $99 annual fee. If Copilot Money reverts to its regular $119 annual price, the annual gap expands to $20. On monthly billing at $9 per month, 12 payments of Copilot Money total $108, which is $1 less than its $109 promotional annual price and $9 more than Tiller."
    },
    {
      "heading": "EveryDollar Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "EveryDollar is a budgeting application created by Ramsey Solutions, built around the principle of giving every dollar a job. EveryDollar offers two plan tiers: a Free version and a Premium version. The Free version costs $0 and includes a monthly budget builder, unlimited spending categories, manual transaction entry, sinking funds, split transactions, and bill due date tracking. EveryDollar Premium begins with a 14-day free trial and costs $79.99 per year when billed annually (equivalent to $6.67 per month), or $17.99 per month when billed monthly. Premium adds automatic bank connectivity, custom budget reports, comma-separated values (CSV) transaction export, financial coach question-and-answer support, and net worth projection modeling.\n\nEveryDollar fits budgeters who want a no-cost tool with manual entry, or who want automated bank feeds backed by financial coach support.\n\nVersus Tiller, EveryDollar Free requires you to log every transaction by hand, whereas Tiller automatically downloads cleared transactions directly into your spreadsheet. EveryDollar Premium introduces automated bank feeds, but restricts customization to pre-set budgeting views rather than freeform spreadsheet tabs and mathematical formulas.\n\nWorked cost example: EveryDollar Free costs $0 per year, saving $99 annually compared to Tiller. EveryDollar Premium on annual billing costs $79.99 per year, which is $19.01 less than Tiller's $99 per year fee. However, EveryDollar Premium on monthly billing costs $17.99 per month, which totals $215.88 over 12 months. That monthly schedule costs $116.88 more per year than Tiller."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Who This Is Not For",
      "body": "This comparison focuses on consumer personal budgeting and money management software. It does not suit several categories of users:\n\n- Business operators who require double-entry bookkeeping, payroll processing, accounts receivable aging, and sales tax filing. Tiller and the consumer apps listed here do not provide formal commercial accounting systems.\n- Individuals who refuse to link banking credentials to third-party aggregation systems and prefer pen-and-paper cash envelopes. EveryDollar Free supports manual entry, but the remaining paid tools rely on automated bank connections.\n- People who want a registered financial professional to manage their investment portfolio directly. None of these applications provide discretionary wealth management or automated trade execution."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Would Change Our Answer on Tiller Alternatives",
      "body": "Published pricing changes and product updates could shift the comparative math between Tiller and these alternatives:\n\n- Tiller adjusting its $99 annual subscription price or altering its 30-day trial and 30-day refund window.\n- Monarch modifying the $99.99 Core or $199.99 Plus annual price points, or ending its promotional discount code.\n- YNAB altering its $109 annual list price, changing its 34-day trial without a card, or restricting its six-person sharing rule.\n- Simplifi publishing a permanent regular 12-month renewal dollar total, or changing its promotional offer terms.\n- Copilot Money ending its $109 promotional annual price to return to $119, or officially releasing its shared Households feature.\n- EveryDollar altering its $79.99 annual or $17.99 monthly Premium pricing."
    }
